{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The decentralized network is fundamentally more resilient to correlated failure than a redundant monolith because it trades 'redundancy' for 'heterogeneity', transforming systemic risks into localized faults.","counterconditions":["The decentralized services share a critical, single-point-of-failure upstream dependency (e.g., a single DNS provider or cloud region).","The 'independence' of the services is illusory due to shared software supply chains (e.g., all services using the same vulnerable library version)."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Operational heterogeneity (as proposed in SRC_007) ensures that identical triggers (e.g., a specific kernel bug or Leap Second event mentioned in SCR_001) do not produce identical failure responses across the network, whereas a monolith with high internal redundancy often employs homogeneous stacks, creating a single point of failure across all redundant nodes.","optional_refs":["df684ec2719d992b5f0eaf0e75b1d3c9f24ba20f171c3376229aa8508fb9119c"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network is *less* resilient than a highly redundant monolith when the network's failure modes are dominated by 'cascading dependencies' rather than 'internal faults'.","counterconditions":["The monolith's internal redundancy is heterogeneous enough to prevent systemic collapse.","The decentralized network employs asynchronous, loosely coupled communication patterns that dampen cascades."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"In a decentralized network, the failure of one small service can trigger a ripple effect across others due to tight API coupling, whereas a monolith can implement strict internal circuit breakers and atomic state recovery that are impossible to coordinate across independent operators (referencing the 'illusory independence' logic in SRC_006).","optional_refs":["192f4dea29c0356232e7fc8b454cbc1fd8d360464265bc6f24cba81bf521e3f8"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The distinction between truly independent failures and apparently uncorrelated failures is identified by the 'Trigger-Response Divergence' across different operational environments.","counterconditions":["The shared cause is so catastrophic that it overrides all operational heterogeneity (e.g., total regional power loss).","The monitoring granularity is too coarse to distinguish between different failure signatures."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"By analyzing the delta between a shared external event (the trigger) and the specific failure signatures (the response) across services, one can determine if a shared cause exists. If the response is uniform despite different environments, the failure is correlated via a shared latent trigger (SCR_001); if responses diverge, the failures are truly independent (SRC_008).","optional_refs":["cd443b133dd273e2ead596e2f099c21a1ce93b674825a910de5afa74229ab656"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Total internal redundancy in a monolith is a 'vanity metric' that masks systemic fragility; the decentralized network's resilience is a function of the intersection of its dependency graphs.","counterconditions":["The decentralized network's services are all hosted on the same underlying infrastructure provider.","The monolith utilizes 'N-version programming' (diverse implementations of the same spec) to achieve true heterogeneity."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"As noted in SCR_003, a monolith with 10,000 identical nodes has an effective redundancy of 1 against any bug that affects that specific binary/kernel combination. The decentralized network's resilience is determined not by the number of services, but by the degree to which their dependency graphs avoid common intersections (SCR_004).","optio